WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Why would a cell surrounded by cholesterol keep making more?

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.400
Brown and Goldstein saw this in skin cells from children with familial

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.720
hypercholesterolemia.

4
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.040
LDL crowded the medium, yet the cells behaved as though cholesterol were scarce.

5
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:23.503
A liver cell gets cholesterol through two supply lines.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:29.523
It can build cholesterol, or pull LDL from blood through LDL receptors.

7
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:33.243
But the cell does not measure LDL outside.

8
00:00:34.563 --> 00:00:38.863
It measures cholesterol reaching its endoplasmic reticulum membrane.

9
00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:45.317
On the import line, an LDL receptor captures LDL at the cell surface.

10
00:00:46.597 --> 00:00:50.897
The particle enters, and its cholesterol joins the cell's internal pool.

11
00:00:52.137 --> 00:00:55.057
A working receptor changes what the gauge can see.

12
00:00:56.117 --> 00:01:01.497
On the synthesis line, acetyl-CoA feeds the long road toward cholesterol.

13
00:01:02.857 --> 00:01:07.697
The main throttle, though not the only one, is HMG-CoA reductase.

14
00:01:08.897 --> 00:01:14.237
It makes mevalonate in a rate-limiting, irreversible step, and statins inhibit it.

15
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:20.207
One ER cholesterol gauge sets both synthesis and LDL uptake.

16
00:01:21.607 --> 00:01:25.767
SCAP is the sensor, and SREBP-2 is the passenger.

17
00:01:27.127 --> 00:01:30.947
When cholesterol binds SCAP, Insig grips SCAP.

18
00:01:32.187 --> 00:01:33.867
The complex stays in the ER.

19
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.450
When ER cholesterol falls, SCAP escapes Insig.

20
00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:46.050
It carries SREBP-2 to the Golgi, where enzymes release its active piece.

21
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:48.870
That piece enters the nucleus.

22
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.950
It raises both HMG-CoA reductase and the LDL receptor.

23
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:58.473
Predict the statin's indirect effect.

24
00:01:59.773 --> 00:02:03.513
If synthesis falls, what happens to LDL receptor number?

25
00:02:04.893 --> 00:02:06.093
Fewer, more, or unchanged?

26
00:02:11.817 --> 00:02:16.437
A statin lowers cholesterol production, so the ER gauge reads low.

27
00:02:18.477 --> 00:02:21.917
SREBP-2 then turns up the LDL-receptor gene.

28
00:02:23.257 --> 00:02:25.657
More receptors pull more LDL from blood.

29
00:02:26.857 --> 00:02:30.977
The drug closes the making line, and the gauge opens the import line.

30
00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:37.237
Familial hypercholesterolemia reveals the same logic from the opposite direction.

31
00:02:38.477 --> 00:02:42.817
With too few working receptors, little LDL cholesterol gets inside.

32
00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:48.877
In the dish, the skin cells' ER gauge stayed low, so synthesis kept running.

33
00:02:50.137 --> 00:02:54.017
In the body, blood LDL rises mainly because clearance is slow.

34
00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:58.707
Follow cholesterol into the ER, and the puzzle dissolves.

35
00:02:59.947 --> 00:03:04.567
A cell responds to cholesterol it can sense, not cholesterol merely nearby.

36
00:03:06.027 --> 00:03:09.467
Low ER cholesterol turns up both making and importing.
